WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 104: The Aroostook Pork and Beans War of 1838: The Bloodless Lumberjack Border War

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:19.920
[Rebecca] Today, we journey into the dense, snow-choked pine forests of northern Maine and New Brunswick to witness one of the most absurd, colorful, and delightfully bloodless international conflicts in military history.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.840
[Rebecca] The Arostook War of 1838 and 39.

5
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:30.560
[Rebecca] Long dubbed the Pork and Beans War, after the staple rations that sustained thousands of freezing militiamen.

6
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.960
[Rebecca] The crisis erupted over 12,000 square miles of disputed virgin timber along the Arustook River.

7
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:50.400
[Rebecca] When Rogue Canadian lumberjacks began illegally poaching prize white pine trees, the state of Maine raised an army, Britain mobilized regulars, and Congress appropriated 10 million dollars and 50,000 troops.

8
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:57.440
[Rebecca] In the end, the two great empires settled their differences over a bottle of fine wine and an amiable game of whist.

9
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.360
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full, hilarious chronicle.

10
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:12.320
[George] The bizarre roots of the Arostook War trace back to the Treaty of Paris of 1783, which formally ended the American Revolutionary War.

11
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:24.240
[George] When British and American diplomats drew the northern boundary between the newly independent United States and British North America, they relied on crude, inaccurate maps of the uncharted wilderness.

12
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.640
[George] The treaty ambiguously stipulated that the border would run from the source of the St.

13
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.560
[George] Croix River along the highlands which divide those rivers that empty themselves into the River Sint.

14
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.160
[George] Lawrence, from those which fall into the Atlantic Ocean.

15
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:45.840
[George] Unfortunately, the geography of northern Maine and western New Brunswick contained no single, obvious mountain ridge.

16
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:53.1000
[George] Instead, it comprised an undulating labyrinth of dense boreal forests, spruce bogs, and interconnected river basins.

17
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:07.400
[George] For over half a century, both nations made conflicting claims to over 12,000 square miles of pristine wilderness in the Arroustock River Valley, an area larger than the entire states of Massachusetts and Connecticut combined.

18
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.520
[George] For Great Britain, the territory was of supreme strategic military importance.

19
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.840
[George] During the winter months, when the St.

20
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:28.520
[George] Lawrence River froze solid, the overland route through the Arroustock Valley was the only direct communication corridor linking the Maritime Naval Base at Halifax, Nova Scotia, with the interior capital of Quebec.

21
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:39.640
[George] For the newly admitted state of Maine, carved out of Massachusetts in 1820, the valley was a commercial gold mine containing the greatest stands of virgin white pine on the continent.

22
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:51.400
[George] Colonial surveyors had attempted to trace the boundary lines through impenetrable spruce swamps and cedar bogs, leaving behind ambiguous field notes that fueled decades of diplomatic acrimony.

23
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:59.800
[George] By the 1830s, the Industrial Revolution and a worldwide shipbuilding boom triggered an insatiable demand for massive timber.

24
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:10.280
[George] The majestic eastern white pines of the Arustook Valley, towering up to 200 feet tall with trunks five feet in diameter, were prized above all other wood on Earth.

25
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:21.400
[George] Known as pumpkin pine for its soft, clear grain, it was ideal for ship masts, wooden hulls, and fine housing construction across Great Britain and the United States.

26
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:31.080
[George] Because the international boundary remained unadjudicated, the Arroustook territory became a lawless, wild frontier populated by rival timber crews.

27
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:44.280
[George] Every winter, as snow and ice made it possible to drag colossal logs across frozen skid roads, hundreds of Canadian lumberjacks from New Brunswick, known colorfully as provincials, marched across the border.

28
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:56.640
[George] Operating with the tacit approval of New Brunswick colonial officials, they clear cut prize timber along the Arostook and Fish Rivers, branding the logs with royal crown marks and floating them down the St.

29
00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.720
[George] John River to British mills.

30
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:08.320
[George] To the citizens and politicians of Maine, this was not merely trespassing, it was blatant international piracy and theft of sovereign American resources.

31
00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:15.360
[George] Maine newspapers erupted in fury, accusing British poachers of robbing the state of millions of dollars in lumber.

32
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:27.120
[George] In Augusta, newly elected Governor John Fairfield declared that Maine had waited fifty years for the federal government in Washington to resolve the border and would tolerate British plunder no longer.

33
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:39.280
[George] Massive white pine trunks cut from the Arustook wilderness were floated down the rushing Spring Rapids, bound into colossal timber rafts that supplied British shipyards constructing Royal Navy men of war.

34
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.920
[George] In January 1839, Governor Fairfield resolved to take decisive law enforcement action.

35
00:04:45.920 --> 00:05:01.600
[George] He appointed Rufus McIntyre, a respected former congressman and state land agent, to lead a civilian posse of 200 armed volunteers into the snowdrifted Arustook wilderness to arrest illegal Canadian lumber crews and confiscate their equipment.

36
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:10.880
[George] McIntyre's posse marched north through Sub Zero blizzards, successfully capturing several Canadian logging camps and impounding thousands of logs.

37
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:27.120
[George] However, on the night of February 12, 1839, while McIntyre and his top aides were sleeping at a remote logging cabin near the confluence of the Little Madawaska and Arostook rivers, a band of forty heavily armed Canadian lumberjacks surrounded the cabin.

38
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:40.680
[George] Kicking down the door, the Canadians dragged McIntyre and his companions out into the snow in their nightclothes, bound them with logging ropes, tossed them into open horse sleighs, and hauled them across the border into New Brunswick.

39
00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:51.160
[George] The captured Maine officials were delivered to the jail in Fredericton, New Brunswick, where colonial Lieutenant Governor Sir John Harvey declared them illegal armed invaders.

40
00:05:51.160 --> 00:06:05.000
[George] Harvey immediately issued an imperial proclamation declaring Maine's posse an act of unlawful invasion, ordered British militia mobilized, and dispatched regular British troops with artillery to take up positions along the border.

41
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:18.920
[George] Rufus McIntyre's civilian posse was authorized to enforce state civil law against foreign timber cartels, but the sudden Canadian midnight raid transformed a local trespass dispute into an international diplomatic crisis.

42
00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:28.680
[George] When news of the land agents' kidnapping reached Augusta on February 15, 1839, the state of Maine exploded in patriotic hysteria.

43
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:41.960
[George] In an emergency session, the state legislature voted unanimously to declare a state of war, appropriating $800,000 and ordering 10,000 state militic men mobilized to march on the frontier.

44
00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:51.720
[George] Across the towns and villages of Maine, Fife and Drum Corps marched through the streets, recruiting volunteers to defend the sovereign honor of the Pine Tree State.

45
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:57.960
[George] Farmers, fishermen and blacksmiths packed their hunting rifles and marched north into the snowy forests.

46
00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:13.240
[George] To sustain the troops through the brutal northern winter, the state quartermasters requisitioned thousands of wooden barrels of salt pork, hardtack crackers, and dried navy beans, prompting soldiers to dub the military campaign the Pork and Beans War.

47
00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:27.600
[George] By early March, over 5,000 main militiamen were encamped along the Arostook River, constructing wooden log fortresses including Fort Fairfield and Fort Kent, mounting brass field guns overlooking the frozen waterways.

48
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:37.760
[George] Across the border, Sir John Harvey deployed 500 British regulars of the 34th and 65th regiments, supported by hundreds of New Brunswick militia.

49
00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:47.600
[George] The two armed camps faced each other across frozen river clearings, muskets loaded, with war between the British Empire and the United States appearing imminent.

50
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:59.840
[George] Volunteers sang rollicking patriotic ballads around northern campfire pits, roasting generous portions of salt pork and navy beans while sharing frontier tales under the sparkling winter stars.

51
00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.280
[George] In Washington, D.C., news of the border crisis sparked national furor.

52
00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:14.000
[George] Maine's congressional representatives demanded immediate federal military intervention, declaring that an attack on Maine was an attack on the entire nation.

53
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:30.160
[George] In a rare display of bipartisan unity, Congress passed legislation authorizing President Martin Van Buren to raise 50,000 federal volunteer troops and appropriated a massive war chest of $10 million, an enormous sum in 1839.

54
00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:44.880
[George] However, President Van Buren and British Prime Minister Lord Melbourne recognized that a full-scale war between the world's greatest naval power and the United States over frozen pine trees would be an absolute economic catastrophe for both nations.

55
00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:55.920
[George] Desperate to defuse the powder keg, Van Buren summoned America's preeminent diplomatic troubleshooter, Major General Winfield Scott, the grand old man of the army.

56
00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:02.200
[George] Standing 6 feet 5 inches tall with an imposing military presence and legendary diplomatic charm.

57
00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:08.040
[George] General Scott was rushed to Maine with broad executive authority to negotiate a truce.

58
00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.160
[George] Scott possessed a crucial diplomatic advantage.

59
00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:24.120
[George] During the War of 1812, he had been captured by the British and treated with remarkable kindness and chivalry by a young British officer who happened to be none other than Sir John Harvey, the current governor of New Brunswick.

60
00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.800
[George] The two opposing commanders were old personal friends.

61
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:39.560
[George] President Van Buren understood that an armed conflict with the British Empire would devastate American international commerce and trigger a catastrophic financial panic in New York and Boston banking houses.

62
00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:50.280
[George] Arriving in Augusta in early March 1839 amidst roaring cheers from patriotic crowds, General Scott immediately began working behind the scenes to cool tempers.

63
00:09:50.280 --> 00:10:02.120
[George] He met privately with Governor Fairfield and legislative leaders, urging caution and warning that an accidental musket discharge on the frontier could drag the nation into a bloody, ruinous conflict.

64
00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:09.720
[George] Simultaneously, Scott opened private, affectionate correspondence with his old friend Sir John Harvey across the border.

65
00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:17.160
[George] Scott appealed to their shared military honor, proposing that both sides agree to an immediate mutual withdrawal of forces.

66
00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:27.960
[George] Maine would withdraw its militia while retaining a small civil posse to prevent timber trespassing, while New Brunswick would withdraw its regular troops and release all American prisoners.

67
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.880
[George] Harvey gladly agreed.

68
00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:38.200
[George] Tradition holds that the terms of the armistice were toasted over a fine dinner accompanied by imported port wine and a friendly game of whist.

69
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:42.920
[George] On March 25, 1839, the formal truce was signed.

70
00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:57.120
[George] The main milishigmen packed their knapsacks, finished their remaining rations of pork and beans, and marched triumphantly back to their hometowns to the tune of Yankee Doodle, having defended the border without firing a single shot in anger.

71
00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:10.160
[George] General Winfield Scott's chivalrous personal relationship with Sir John Harvey demonstrated the immense power of mutual military respect and gentlemanly integrity in resolving international crises peacefully.

72
00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:25.280
[George] Three years later, in 1842, the disputed border was permanently settled in Washington through the historic Webster-Rashburton Treaty, negotiated by United States Secretary of State Daniel Webster and British diplomat Lord Ashburton.

73
00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:27.280
[George] Under the Compromise Agreement.

74
00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:32.400
[George] The disputed territory of 12,000 square miles was partitioned amicably.

75
00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:47.200
[George] The United States received 7,000 square miles, giving Maine its modern northern boundary, while Great Britain retained 5,000 square miles, securing its vital overland military corridor between Halifax and Quebec.

76
00:11:47.200 --> 00:12:00.400
[George] Remarkably, throughout months of armed mobilization involving over 10,000 heavily armed soldiers facing each other in tense winter conditions, the official casualty count of the Arustock War was zero battle deaths.

77
00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:17.520
[George] Several milishigmen tragically died of measles, pneumonia, or hypothermia in winter camps, but the only combat casualty in the entire war was an unfortunate black pig that strayed across a logging trail near Fort Fairfield and was accidentally shot by an over-eager Maine sentinel.

78
00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:29.440
[George] The Arustock War remains celebrated in Maine and Canadian folklore as a rare, shining triumph of common sense, diplomacy, and personal friendship over the destructive madness of war.

79
00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:46.600
[George] Today, along the peaceful, friendly border between Maine and New Brunswick, the original wooden blockhouse of Fort Kent stands preserved as a national historic landmark, a whimsical reminder of the Great Pork and Beans War and the day two mighty empires chose peace over Pine.

80
00:12:46.600 --> 00:13:00.040
[George] The successful Webster Rashburton Compromise established the longest undefended international border on Earth, inaugurating two centuries of enduring friendship and mutual cooperation between two great nations.

81
00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:10.840
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

82
00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:18.280
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we sail into stormy Penobscot Bay for the surreal disaster of the Royal Tar Circus.

